# String literals written in two or more crates.
#
# The constitution names this class outright — "an emitter and a consumer of
# the same literal" — and it is the class that produced COOK_CMD_FAILED and
# REGISTER_SURFACE_NAME before anyone was looking for it. A shared literal is a
# wire format whether or not anybody called it one, and the two ends agree only
# by luck until one of them is edited.
#
# This file is a to-do list, not a settlement. Most entries below are debt, not
# deliberate copies, and each says which. Deleting an entry is how the work
# gets marked done: the gate fails if a waiver stops matching anything.
#
# One finding from the rule's first run is absent because it was fixed rather
# than waived. cook-cli re-typed "set -e\n" to strip it, next to a
# cook-contracts constant whose own doc says callers must derive the prefix
# from it rather than re-type it — a rule already written down and already
# broken. It now reads SET_E_PREFIX.

# --- The two Lua VMs' door names -------------------------------------------
#
# This section held ten entries and holds none. Nine were the door names and
# metamethods COOK-439 was filed for — add_unit, dep_output, dep_output_list,
# member_to_string, prior_outputs, interactive, step_group and the
# __metatable/__newindex pair — plus `cook.member_to_string: {e}`, the
# diagnostic that travelled with its door. `discovered_inputs` went with them
# from the wire-format section below, where COOK-421 had narrowed it to exactly
# the half that was a door-field spelling.
#
# CS-0213 gave the names one home in cook_contracts::registration, the
# member_to_string door one implementation in cook-lua-stdlib, and the
# read-only `var` seal — the policy those two metamethod names were standing in
# for — one implementation there too. The step_group entry's third end,
# cook-graph's renderer, reads DepKind::wire_name.
#
# Two things about that are worth keeping, because neither is in the rule.
#
# The waiver for the __metatable/__newindex pair said the names are Lua's own
# and cannot drift, and then listed them anyway "because both sites are sealing
# a table against writes, and THAT policy is a decision with two
# implementations". It was right, and it is the only entry this file has had
# whose key was never the finding. A rule that reads text can only ever name a
# symptom; the justification is where the finding gets said.
#
# The cache.scope clone in cross-crate-clones.jsonl (now also gone) was
# described as ALREADY divergent — one setter writes, the other raises. That
# reading was wrong in a way worth recording: the setter difference is CS-0074,
# specified, and correct in both phases. Nothing had rotted. The finding was the
# other way round: the nine tenths that had to agree were the copied part, and
# the one tenth that was meant to differ was invisible inside the copy.
# install_probes_api takes both operations as arguments, so the agreement is
# structural and the difference is legible at the call site.
#
# What this rule still cannot see, said out loud: cook-luagen EMITS several of
# these doors inside multi-line templates far longer than any literal the rule
# compares. The dep_output family now composes through
# registration::door_call. The ones whose argument is a Lua expression --
# cook.add_unit{...}, cook.member_to_string(item), cook.step_group(function()
# ... — keep their template text, and that copy carries the deliberate-copy
# protocol's third condition: an agreement test,
# cook-luagen/src/tests/door_names_tests.rs, which fails if a door is renamed
# in cook-contracts while a template here keeps the old spelling.

# --- Cache and log wire formats --------------------------------------------
# A field name written by one crate and read by another is a wire format. These
# cross the on-disk boundary, so a rename on one side does not fail to compile:
# it silently stops matching, and a cache that stops matching is the worst
# failure this build system has.
{"key": "dep_order", "sites": ["crates/cook-graph/src/dag_data.rs:115", "crates/cook-register/src/context.rs:342"], "why": "Debt: a registration field name spelled by the producer and by the graph that renders it."}
{"key": "exit_code", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/test_reporter/mod.rs:284", "crates/cook-progress/src/log_reader.rs:92"], "why": "Debt: a JSON field written by the test reporter and parsed by the log reader. The two ends are a serializer and its inverse, which the admission bar names as qualifying on its own."}
{"key": "should_fail", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/test_reporter/mod.rs:278", "crates/cook-lang/src/disposition.rs:202"], "why": "Debt: the disposition name the parser produces and the reporter serialises."}
{"key": "schema_version", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/test_reporter/mod.rs:255", "crates/cook-graph/src/lib.rs:52"], "why": "Deliberate: two unrelated JSON schemas each version themselves, and the field name is a convention rather than an agreement between them. Listed because if the two ever describe one document, this is where that mistake will show first. CS-0217 tested that clause and it held. Adding a version to `cook why --unit --format json` would have put a THIRD spelling in cook-cli that did describe cook-graph's document, so the key and the number moved behind one `cook_graph::stamp_schema_version` that both `cook why` documents call, and neither renderer spells the key. What is left is what this line always claimed: cook-cli's remaining site is the test report's own schema, which shares nothing with the graph's but the word. It ends if the test report and the why document ever merge, or if a third document appears that is neither."}

# --- Diagnostics with two authors ------------------------------------------
# A message written in two places is one message until somebody improves one of
# them. These are user-visible text, so the drift is silent and public.
{"key": "cook: warning: {warning}", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs:843", "crates/cook-register/src/engine.rs:2040"], "why": "Debt: the warning prefix — the shape of every warning cook prints — with two authors. This is a rendering rule, which the admission bar admits by name."}
{"key": "cycle among recipes: {unresolved:?}", "sites": ["crates/cook-contracts/src/unit_graph/mod.rs:153", "crates/cook-engine/src/dag_builder.rs:37"], "why": "Debt, and a signal: the same cycle diagnostic in the unit-graph law and in the engine's DAG builder means the cycle CHECK is also implemented twice. The message is the visible half."}
{"key": "unknown recipe: {0}", "sites": ["crates/cook-engine/src/lib.rs:484", "crates/cook-plan/src/analyzer.rs:20"], "why": "Debt: one error a user can reach down two code paths, worded identically today by luck."}
{"key": "invalid .cook/cloud.toml: {e}", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/cache_du.rs:29", "crates/cook-engine/src/run.rs:677"], "why": "Debt: one config file, one failure, two authors — and the path inside the message is itself a layout fact that cook-contracts already owns elsewhere."}
{"key": "terminal_outputs mutex poisoned", "sites": ["crates/cook-plan/src/registers.rs:336", "crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs:72"], "why": "Debt: an expect() message duplicated across the two crates that lock the same map. Low stakes for a reader, but it marks one lock invariant owned in two places."}
{"key": "probe '{}': {}", "sites": ["crates/cook-execute/src/pool.rs:1382", "crates/cook-probe/src/eval/mod.rs:95"], "why": "Debt: the probe-error prefix, written by the evaluator and again by the worker VM that surfaces it."}
{"key": "env changed", "sites": ["crates/cook-cache/src/check.rs:133", "crates/cook-engine/src/executor.rs:823"], "why": "Debt: a cache-miss cause string produced in two crates. Miss causes are what `cook why` renders, so this is a vocabulary with two authors."}
{"key": "working_dir lock", "sites": ["crates/cook-lua-stdlib/src/lib.rs:99", "crates/cook-execute/src/pool.rs:351"], "why": "Debt: an expect() message on the same lock, held in two crates."}

# --- Surface vocabulary -----------------------------------------------------
{"key": "ingredients", "sites": ["crates/cook-lang/src/cook_line.rs:255", "crates/cook-luagen/src/cook_step.rs:249", "crates/cook-register/src/capture.rs:97"], "why": "Debt, and the highest-stakes entry here: a Cookfile keyword spelled by the parser, by codegen and by registration. The Standard defines it; the grammar qualifies for cook-contracts by name; three crates spelling it is three chances to disagree with the spec."}
{"key": "Cookfile", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/cli.rs:36", "crates/cook-plan/src/entry.rs:59", "crates/cook-register/src/engine.rs:232"], "why": "Debt: the entry-file name, which is a layout fact. cook-contracts already owns a layout module; this belongs in it."}
{"key": "function", "sites": ["crates/cook-lang/src/recipe.rs:90", "crates/cook-register/src/probe_api.rs:265"], "why": "Deliberate: two unrelated uses of an English word — the parser naming a Lua construct, the probe API describing an expected value type. Nothing agrees with anything here; the rule cannot tell an English word from a wire format, and this is the cost of that."}
{"key": "probe:{key}", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs:2555", "crates/cook-probe/src/eval/mod.rs:530"], "why": "Debt: a cache-key prefix composed by the evaluator and reconstructed by the CLI. A key format with two authors is a cross-machine cache hazard, which is the worst failure mode this repo has."}

# --- Formatting coincidences ------------------------------------------------
{"key": "    {}\\n", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs:2274", "crates/cook-luagen/src/recipe.rs:968"], "why": "Deliberate: two unrelated indent-and-newline format strings. Four spaces is not a shared decision, and merging them would couple a diagnostic renderer to a code generator for no reason."}
{"key": "exit {c}", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/test_reporter/failures.rs:59", "crates/cook-logs/src/render/picker.rs:20"], "why": "Debt, mild: two renderers spelling how a non-zero exit is shown to a user. Rendering rules are admissible law by the bar's own list, and these two are already one sentence apart from disagreeing."}
